"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 6.6 and the pathological T3a stage with extraprostatic extension documented in the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the initial biopsy are missing.\n\nThe prognosis would be significantly revised by obtaining postoperative PSA levels and tracking their subsequent trajectory. Furthermore, definitive nodal sampling results would provide crucial information regarding regional disease extent. Given the current data, the uncertainty surrounding nodal status remains a key factor impacting prognostic certainty. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.64–97.15 months; its coverage has not been validated."
